  "slug": "quantum-spin-96c3",
  "summary": "Four preprints offer design predictions for phase-coherent superconducting transport, none yet backed by experimental demonstration.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is phase-coherent quantum transport, in which superconducting or Josephson elements control charge, spin and thermal responses. One preprint derives a lower bound on current noise in coherent electron transport via Floquet-Nambu scattering and a Cramér-Rao inequality. A numerical study proposes a flux-driven DC-SQUID threshold detector with nonequilibrium phase dynamics to reduce thermal noise and detect microwave pulses near the single-microwave-photon energy scale. A two-orbital quantum dot Josephson junction with Rashba spin-orbit coupling and Zeeman splitting maps a temperature difference onto a phase difference for thermometry. A theory proposal argues that the inverse spin Hall effect in a superconductor could approach unity spin-to-charge conversion in a nonlocal spin-valve geometry. All four items are preprints and theoretical or numerical proposals, so the new content consists of design predictions rather than experimental demonstrations.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
